---
/* THE FORENAMES, 23 November 2026 (the estate's «The forenames» in the menu group The name; menus.md). The kit's SayYourName, reading Record Atlas's given-name catalogue.
 *
 * THE FORMS ARE NOT KEPT HERE. Every form of a given name, by language, and whether a family archive has met it on a record, is kept ONCE, in Record Atlas
 * (data/given-name-families.json, written only through scripts/given_name_variants.py), and fetched into src/data/given-names.json by the Archive Kit's
 * tools/atlas_given_names.py with --seen src/data/given-forms-seen.json (this archive's own count of every form in its records; tools/build-given-forms-seen.py)
 * and --strict (it exits 2 for a form of ours the Atlas lacks). `npm run given:atlas` does it; `npm run check:forenames` is the offline gate. A form met in a record
 * here is added to the Atlas with its evidence, never to this page.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE CHOOSES, AND SAYS. The Atlas holds the name in ~60 languages; a page for a Uruguayan and Argentine register with an English branch shows the eight
 * that bear on these records (LANGS below), and the rows in which this archive has met a form in one of them. The Atlas's `seen` is set from a form's spelling
 * alone, so a form written the same in several languages («Maria», «Anna») would be «attested here» in each. Here a form met in these records is attributed to the
 * FIRST of these languages (in this order) that writes it, and to no other; the Atlas's own count across archives stays as `seenAll`.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SayYourName from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/SayYourName.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import G from "../data/given-names.json";
import seenFile from "../data/given-forms-seen.json";

/* Spanish and Latin are the languages of the registers; English is the Taylor and Brayley line and the South African records; Italian, Portuguese, Catalan,
   Galician and Basque are the neighbours a Spanish or River Plate clerk's name could have come from or been written in. */
const LANGS = ["spanish", "latin", "english", "italian", "portuguese", "catalan", "galician", "basque"];
const fold = (s) => String(s || "").toLowerCase().normalize("NFD").replace(/[̀-ͯ]/g, "").trim().replace(/\?+$/, "");

const langs = LANGS.map((k) => G.languages.find((l) => l.key === k)).filter(Boolean);
const rows = [];
for (const r of G.rows) {
  const claimed = new Set();
  const out = {};
  let here = 0;
  for (const k of LANGS) {
    out[k] = (r.langs?.[k] || []).map((f) => {
      const key = fold(f.form);
      const mine = f.seen > 0 && !claimed.has(key);
      if (mine) { claimed.add(key); here++; }
      return { ...f, seen: mine ? f.seen : 0 };
    });
  }
  if (here > 0) rows.push({ ...r, langs: out });
}
rows.sort((a, b) => a.canon.localeCompare(b.canon, "es"));

const heldRows = new Set(G.rows.flatMap((r) => Object.values(r.langs || {}).flat().map((f) => fold(f.form))));
const heldNotSame = new Set(G.rows.flatMap((r) => (r.notSame || []).map((f) => fold(f.form))));
const observed = seenFile.observed || {};
const forms = Object.keys(observed);
const total = rows.reduce((a, r) => a + Object.values(r.langs).reduce((b, f) => b + f.length, 0), 0);
const met = rows.reduce((a, r) => a + Object.values(r.langs).reduce((b, f) => b + f.filter((x) => x.seen > 0).length, 0), 0);
const inCatalogue = forms.filter((f) => heldRows.has(fold(f))).length;
const differentName = forms.filter((f) => !heldRows.has(fold(f)) && heldNotSame.has(fold(f)));
---
<Base title="The forenames"
      eyebrow={`${forms.length} forms of given names met in these records · ${rows.length} names`}
      dek="A person is written down by clerks in different languages and different centuries, and a given name is where it shows first: Juan, Joannes, John and Giovanni are one name. Type one and see every form the same name would have been entered under.">

  <section class="prose">
    <p>This archive's registers are in Spanish and in the Latin of the church, its English branch in English, and the clerks did not agree. <em>Joaquin</em> is
      written <em>Joaquín</em> or <em>Joachim</em> or <em>Joaquim</em>; <em>Isabel</em> is <em>Ysabel</em>; a Juan in a Montevideo register is Joannes in the
      Latin of the entry and John on an English one. A search typed from a family tree finds one of those and misses the rest, and a nil from that search is only a
      nil about the one spelling.</p>
    <p><strong>A name you have looked up is a link you can send.</strong> The address bar follows the search, so <span class="mono" style="font-size:13px">?n=Joaquin</span> opens on
      exactly what you were looking at.</p>
    <p><strong>Two kinds of cell.</strong> A form printed plainly is one this archive has met in its own records: <strong>{met}</strong> of the {total} below. A form marked
      <span class="aid">like this<sup>°</sup></span> is a <strong>lookup aid</strong>: the right form in that language, and the right thing to type into an index tomorrow, but not
      something these records have yet shown. The difference between «we have read this» and «this is what to look for» is the difference between a record and a lead, and the page
      never merges the two.</p>
    <p><strong>Nothing here is decided on this page.</strong> The forms are kept once, with the evidence that each was met, in <a href="https://recordatlas.org/your-name/">Record Atlas</a>,
      and this page is read from that catalogue. Of the {forms.length} forms of given names this archive has met, <strong>{inCatalogue}</strong> are held in it as forms of a name. {forms.length - inCatalogue - differentName.length > 0 ? `${forms.length - inCatalogue - differentName.length} are not yet; ` : ""}The Atlas
      keeps the name in many more languages than the eight shown here (the ones these records bear on: Spanish, Latin, English, Italian, Portuguese, Catalan, Galician and Basque), and every
      one of them is on the Atlas's own page.</p>
    <p>The register writes names without accents (<em>Joaquin</em>, <em>Concepcion</em>), and a search here ignores them too. A form met in several of these languages is counted as met in the first of
      them in the order above; the others stay as lookup aids.</p>
    {differentName.length > 0 && (
      <p><strong>{differentName.length === 1 ? "One form" : `${differentName.length} forms`} in these records the Atlas holds as a different name from the one it files near it:</strong>
        {" "}{differentName.map((f) => f[0].toUpperCase() + f.slice(1)).join(", ")}. The Atlas's page for the name each could be taken for says so (&laquo;Look alike, but a different name&raquo;), and this archive does not file them under that name either. They are not searchable here, because the Atlas keeps one family for a form.</p>
    )}
  </section>

  <SayYourName rows={rows} u={u} langs={langs}
    chips={["Juan", "Joannes", "Joaquin", "Jose", "Ysabel", "Maria", "Eduviges", "Bernabe"]} />

  <section class="prose" style="margin-top:48px">
    <h3>The forms this does not cover</h3>
    <p>Diminutives and household names (<em>Pepe</em>, <em>Paco</em>, <em>Nene</em>) appear in speech and rarely in a register. A person who bore two or three forenames is counted once under each:
      the register's «Maria Luisa» is one Maria and one Luisa. A given name that a record gives to a person who is not of the name, such as a witness, a godparent or an official, is counted
      too; <em>what</em> a form was met in is the Atlas's evidence line, and nothing about who bore it is kept anywhere.</p>
    <h3>And the surname does the same thing</h3>
    <p>The spellings of the surname move with the clerk and the place, not with the bloodline, and they are kept the same way: once, in Record Atlas, not here. See <a href={u("/name/")}>The Name</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  p .aid { color: var(--ink-3); font-style: italic; }
  p .aid sup { color: var(--terra); font-style: normal; }
</style>
